{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LjMggqaKXvxFkRHcFesxxy","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd024-expand\n     2→title: \"cmd/expand: Convert Tabs to Spaces\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  macOS BSD expand supports only a single tab stop interval, while GNU expand supports\n     6→  arbitrary tab stop lists. Scripts that align columnar data with custom tab stops\n     7→  depend on the GNU behavior. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference\n     8→  binary (gexpand, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations,\n     9→  verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    10→\n    11→goals:\n    12→  - G1: Replace tab characters in each input file with the appropriate number of spaces to reach the next tab stop, matching GNU expand behavior.\n    13→  - G2: Support -t to configure tab stop positions as a single interval or a comma-separated list of absolute positions.\n    14→  - G3: Pass non-tab characters through unchanged, preserving newlines and other bytes.\n    15→\n    16→requirements:\n    17→  R1:\n    18→    title: Default tab expansion (tab stop every 8 columns)\n    19→    items:\n    20→      - R1.1: By default, tab characters must be replaced by enough spaces to advance to the next multiple-of-8 column position. Column positions are 1-indexed; a tab at column 1 advances to column 9.\n    21→      - R1.2: Multiple consecutive tabs must each advance to the next tab stop independently.\n    22→      - R1.3: Non-tab characters must be written unchanged. Each non-tab byte (including multi-byte UTF-8 sequences under LC_ALL=C) counts as one column.\n    23→      - R1.4: Newline characters reset the column position to 1 for the next line.\n    24→\n    25→  R2:\n    26→    title: Custom tab stops (-t)\n    27→    items:\n    28→      - R2.1: \"-t N must set a uniform tab stop interval of N columns (equivalent to tab stops at N, 2N, 3N, ...). N must be a positive integer.\"\n    29→      - R2.2: \"-t LIST must set tab stops at the absolute column positions given in LIST (comma-separated or space-separated positive integers in strictly increasing order). A tab at or past the last explicit tab stop is replaced by a single space.\"\n    30→      - R2.3: The -t option replaces the default tab stop interval of 8. If -t is given multiple times, the last value takes effect.\n    31→      - R2.4: When LIST contains a single value, it behaves identically to -t N (uniform interval).\n    32→\n    33→  R3:\n    34→    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    35→    items:\n    36→      - R3.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n    37→      - R3.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr. Processing continues for remaining files.\n    38→      - R3.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n    39→      - R3.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n    40→\n    41→  R4:\n    42→    title: Differential testing\n    43→    items:\n    44→      - R4.1: Differential tests must compare Go expand output against gexpand byte-for-byte via pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests.\n    45→      - R4.2: \"Tests must cover: default tab expansion, -t N with a single interval, -t LIST with multiple tab stops, input with no tabs (passthrough), and multiple tabs in succession.\"\n    46→      - R4.3: Tests must use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent divergence.\n    47→\n    48→non_goals:\n    49→  - cmd/expand does not process backspace characters for column position adjustment (GNU expand does not adjust for backspaces either under LC_ALL=C).\n    50→  - cmd/expand does not implement Unicode grapheme cluster width; each byte counts as one column under LC_ALL=C.\n    51→\n    52→acceptance_criteria:\n    53→  - \"AC1: printf 'a\\\\tb' | expand produces 'a       b' (7 spaces, total 8 columns), matching gexpand.\"\n    54→  - \"AC2: printf 'a\\\\tb' | expand -t 4 produces 'a   b' (3 spaces), matching gexpand -t 4.\"\n    55→  - \"AC3: printf 'a\\\\tb\\\\tc' | expand -t 1,5,9 expands each tab to the correct absolute position, matching gexpand -t 1,5,9.\"\n    56→  - \"AC4: Input with no tabs passes through unchanged, matching gexpand.\"\n    57→  - \"AC5: Differential test against gexpand passes for all required test cases.\"\n    58→  - \"AC6: cmd/expand compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    59→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a7c2a9f-55e1-4be3-bdbc-961046b78ffd","uuid":"7ab286bf-66e6-4b1b-842c-7432f5046e4f","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/351/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd024-expand.yaml","content":"id: prd024-expand\ntitle: \"cmd/expand: Convert Tabs to Spaces\"\n\nproblem: |\n  macOS BSD expand supports only a single tab stop interval, while GNU expand supports\n  arbitrary tab stop lists. Scripts that align columnar data with custom tab stops\n  depend on the GNU behavior. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference\n  binary (gexpand, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations,\n  verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Replace tab characters in each input file with the appropriate number of spaces to reach the next tab stop, matching GNU expand behavior.\n  - G2: Support -t to configure tab stop positions as a single interval or a comma-separated list of absolute positions.\n  - G3: Pass non-tab characters through unchanged, preserving newlines and other bytes.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default tab expansion (tab stop every 8 columns)\n    items:\n      - R1.1: By default, tab characters must be replaced by enough spaces to advance to the next multiple-of-8 column position. Column positions are 1-indexed; a tab at column 1 advances to column 9.\n      - R1.2: Multiple consecutive tabs must each advance to the next tab stop independently.\n      - R1.3: Non-tab characters must be written unchanged. Each non-tab byte (including multi-byte UTF-8 sequences under LC_ALL=C) counts as one column.\n      - R1.4: Newline characters reset the column position to 1 for the next line.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Custom tab stops (-t)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: \"-t N must set a uniform tab stop interval of N columns (equivalent to tab stops at N, 2N, 3N, ...). N must be a positive integer.\"\n      - R2.2: \"-t LIST must set tab stops at the absolute column positions given in LIST (comma-separated or space-separated positive integers in strictly increasing order). A tab at or past the last explicit tab stop is replaced by a single space.\"\n      - R2.3: The -t option replaces the default tab stop interval of 8. If -t is given multiple times, the last value takes effect.\n      - R2.4: When LIST contains a single value, it behaves identically to -t N (uniform interval).\n\n  R3:\n    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    items:\n      - R3.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R3.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr. Processing continues for remaining files.\n      - R3.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n      - R3.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Differential testing\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Differential tests must compare Go expand output against gexpand byte-for-byte via pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests.\n      - R4.2: \"Tests must cover: default tab expansion, -t N with a single interval, -t LIST with multiple tab stops, input with no tabs (passthrough), and multiple tabs in succession.\"\n      - R4.3: Tests must use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent divergence.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/expand does not process backspace characters for column position adjustment (GNU expand does not adjust for backspaces either under LC_ALL=C).\n  - cmd/expand does not implement Unicode grapheme cluster width; each byte counts as one column under LC_ALL=C.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: printf 'a\\\\tb' | expand produces 'a       b' (7 spaces, total 8 columns), matching gexpand.\"\n  - \"AC2: printf 'a\\\\tb' | expand -t 4 produces 'a   b' (3 spaces), matching gexpand -t 4.\"\n  - \"AC3: printf 'a\\\\tb\\\\tc' | expand -t 1,5,9 expands each tab to the correct absolute position, matching gexpand -t 1,5,9.\"\n  - \"AC4: Input with no tabs passes through unchanged, matching gexpand.\"\n  - \"AC5: Differential test against gexpand passes for all required test cases.\"\n  - \"AC6: cmd/expand compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":59,"startLine":1,"totalLines":59}}}
